```

To fix this, you can use the `promise.all` function
```js
Promise.all(fetch(url1), fetch(url2)) //pass it promise objects
```
This isn't really ideal 

```js
//Promise.all returns an array of resolves
const resloves = Promise.all(
				fetch(url1).then(response => response.json()), 
				fetch(url2).then(response => response.json())
				);
//resolves[0] -> results data
//resolves[1] -> qulifying data
```
We do *not* need to know this for the exam, but this is a nice thing we can use.
